    "passage": "Vertical Farms Take Root in Urban Warehouses\n\nOnce dismissed as an expensive experiment, vertical farming is becoming a practical part of the food supply in several large cities. GreenAxis Farms, which converted a former appliance warehouse in Deltona into a four-story growing facility two years ago, now supplies lettuce, basil, and kale to more than sixty restaurants and a regional supermarket chain. Because crops are grown indoors under LED lights, the farm operates year-round regardless of weather and uses about ninety percent less water than conventional outdoor fields. Produce also travels shorter distances; deliveries reach most customers within hours of harvest. Nonetheless, challenges remain. Electricity accounts for nearly a third of GreenAxis's operating costs, and the company has so far limited production to leafy greens, which grow quickly and command relatively high prices. \"Grains and root vegetables simply are not profitable indoors yet,\" said operations manager Felix Marsh. The company is testing solar panels on the warehouse roof to lower its utility expenses. If the trial succeeds, GreenAxis plans to open two additional facilities in neighboring states by 2028.",
